HPA vs VPA in Kubernetes

Scaling ka matlab sirf zyada pods launch karna nahi hota.
Kabhi-kabhi ek pod ko aur resources dena padta hai.
Kubernetes me 2 tarike ke autoscaling options hote hain:
HPA – Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
VPA – Vertical Pod Autoscaler

Let’s simplify them 
1. HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)
Use when: "Traffic zyada aa raha hai? Zyada pods chahiye!"
HPA automatically increases or decreases number of pods based on resource usage.


Kaise kaam karta hai?
Kubernetes watches metrics like CPU and memory.
Jaise hi CPU usage 70% se upar gaya → HPA ek aur pod launch karega.

minReplicas: 2
maxReplicas: 10
targetCPUUtilizationPercentage: 70


Example:
 Pizza app pe lunch time me traffic spike aaya.
      HPA: “Zyada log aa gaye bhai, aur chef (pods) bulao!”


2. VPA (Vertical Pod Autoscaler)
Use when: "Pod chal toh raha hai, but thoda kamzor hai – resources badhao!"
VPA increases the resources (CPU/Memory) of a pod instead of increasing pod count.

Kaise kaam karta hai?
Kubernetes checks how much resource each pod is actually using.
If a pod is crashing because it needs more memory, VPA adjusts the resource requests and limits.

updateMode: "Auto"


Example:
Pizza app ka ek pod bar-bar crash ho raha hai due to memory limit.
    VPA: “Is pod ko aur taqat do — memory badhao!”
    Real-world Scenario: Pizza API

You deployed a pizza-api:
     During lunch, traffic goes up suddenly.
     HPA launches more pods to handle the load.
     One of the pods keeps getting OOMKilled (Out of Memory).
     VPA increases memory request to avoid future crashes.
Matlab HPA crowd control karta hai, aur VPA body ko strong banata hai 




Interview Time Answer:
Q: What's the difference between HPA and VPA in Kubernetes?
 A:
 .  HPA adjusts number of pods
    VPA adjusts resources (CPU/Memory) of a pod


Important Note:
You shouldn’t run HPA and VPA together on the same deployment.
Why? One is changing pod count, other is changing pod size — conflict ho sakta hai.

